editorialMd:
Build risingLengths

A subsequence keeps numbers in their original order but may skip some numbers. For each position in `readings`, find the longest strictly increasing subsequence that ends at that position. Strictly increasing means each next number must be larger; equal numbers do not count as an increase. Record the length, not the chosen numbers. Put one length per position in `risingLengths`.

Return the answer

Add up every number in `risingLengths`. Store the sum in `total`. Complete `risingLabelsTotalReceipt` so it returns `total`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `risingLengths` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function risingLabelsTotalReceipt(readings) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  const tails=[];for(const x of a){let l=0,r=tails.length;while(l<r){const m=(l+r)>>1;if(tails[m]<x)l=m+1;else r=m;}tails[l]=x;out.push(l+1);}
  return out.reduce((s,x)=>s+x,0);
}


